  "A smaller, faster copy of data kept nearer the reader, so repeated reads avoid the source. It is a bet on two properties of the access pattern: temporal locality, and skew — a small fraction of keys taking most of the reads. The arithmetic makes the bet visible: a cache sized at 20% of the dataset over uniformly random access returns a 20% hit rate, while the same 20% over a Zipf-distributed workload can return 95%. So it does nothing for uniform access over a large keyspace, does not make writes faster, does not fix a slow query, and is by definition allowed to lose everything at any moment.",
