MESSAGING = [
Q("basic",
  "You move email sending behind a queue whose vendor advertises exactly-once delivery. A user reports receiving the same email four times. Who is wrong?",
  "whether they know exactly-once does not exist end to end, and whether they can locate the exact boundary at which the guarantee stops",
  "Not the vendor — the design, about what that guarantee covers. Exactly-once does not exist end to end. What is sold as exactly-once is at-least-once plus deduplication *inside* the broker's boundary; the moment an effect leaves that boundary — an SMTP call, a card charge — the acknowledgement can be lost after the work succeeded, and the message comes back. Four deliveries means the worker died or timed out after sending and before acking, three times. The fix is an idempotent consumer, and checking that the ack happens after the work is durable rather than before.",
  ["blames the vendor",
   "believes a better broker would give true exactly-once",
   "adds retries without mentioning idempotency",
   "cannot say where the guarantee's boundary lies"],
  [("Then why not just acknowledge before processing?",
    "Because that converts at-least-once into at-most-once and silently loses work on every crash. It is a one-line change and a data-loss bug. The crash window is exactly the same size either way — what changes is which side of the ack the durable copy sits on, and only one of those is recoverable. It is also the invisible one: no error, no retry, no metric, and queue depth looks perfectly healthy."),
   ("How does the broker know a worker has died?",
    "It does not. Receiving a message makes it invisible for a visibility timeout; only the ack deletes it. Nothing observable distinguishes a crashed worker from a slow one — both are silence. So the timeout is a bet: set it below your slowest job and you manufacture duplicates on healthy workers, set it far above and a genuinely dead worker's message sits undelivered for that long.")],
  "06-messaging/queues/"),

Q("basic",
  "An internal call takes 5 ms and succeeds 99.99% of the time. A colleague proposes putting it behind a queue. Do you?",
  "whether they will decline asynchrony — most material's reflex answer is queue it, and here that is wrong",
  "No. A queue is a distributed system's worth of complexity — a broker to run, two more failure modes, duplicate and ordering semantics to reason about — for work that finishes in 5 ms and essentially always succeeds. It also makes the answer eventual, so if a human is waiting on the result you have made the experience worse rather than better. Queue work that is slow, that can be retried later, or whose result the caller does not need. This is none of those.",
  ["queues it because asynchronous sounds more scalable",
   "no account of what a broker costs to run and operate",
   "cannot name a case where a synchronous call is the right answer",
   "treats the queue as free decoupling"],
  [("Where is the line? What would make you queue it?",
    "When the work is slow enough that a user is waiting on something they do not need — sending email, generating a PDF, re-encoding video — or when it must survive the caller going away. And when you do reach for one, SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED on a Postgres table is a perfectly good queue up to a few thousand jobs a second and removes an entire component from the architecture."),
   ("You add a queue and it absorbs your traffic spike beautifully. What has it not done?",
    "Added capacity. A queue defers work; it does not create throughput. If producers sustainably outpace consumers the queue grows without bound until memory or disk runs out, and you have converted a slowdown into an outage. It buys time to absorb a spike, never a permanent deficit.")],
  "06-messaging/queues/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "What signal do you autoscale a worker fleet on?",
  "whether they know CPU is the standard wrong answer for I/O-bound consumers, and whether they pair depth with a rate comparison",
  "Queue depth, paired with processing rate against arrival rate. CPU tells you how hard the existing workers are working; depth tells you whether the fleet is keeping up. A worker fleet at 20-30% CPU with a growing backlog needs more workers, and a CPU-based policy will never add them — the work is I/O-bound, which is the normal case, so by its own signal the fleet is idle. Alert on message *age* as well as depth: ten messages three hours old is a worse signal than ten thousand one second old, and a depth-only alert misses it entirely.",
  ["says CPU",
   "no comparison of arrival rate against processing rate",
   "alerts on depth alone",
   "cannot explain why an I/O-bound fleet looks idle"],
  [("Depth is the right signal, and it is also what aims the fleet at your database. Explain.",
    "After a backlog builds, depth is enormous, so the policy adds workers until it is not — the fleet is now sized by the backlog rather than by anything downstream can absorb. Meanwhile the queue removed the natural backpressure the request path had: on the request path a slow database slows the client, which throttles arrival, and behind a queue nothing pushes back at all."),
   ("Give me the number that decides whether the database survives.",
    "Total fleet concurrency: workers multiplied by per-worker concurrency. Twenty workers at concurrency 50 is a thousand simultaneous operations, and nobody ever configured a thousand — two people configured 20 and 50, in two different files, probably in different quarters. Bound it against measured downstream capacity and give workers their own connection pool."),
   ("You must drain 500,000 messages in an hour and each job takes 200 ms. Size it.",
    "500,000 over 3,600 seconds is about 139 jobs a second; Little's Law gives 139 x 0.2, so roughly 28 concurrent slots across the whole fleet. The arithmetic is the easy half — the ceiling is set by what the dependency can absorb rather than by what the backlog demands, and if that means the drain takes three hours then it takes three hours.")],
  "06-messaging/workers/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "A colleague says the pipeline obviously needs ordered delivery. Price it for me.",
  "whether they know global ordering and parallel consumption are mutually exclusive, and can name what the compromise actually costs",
  "Global ordering and parallel consumption are mutually exclusive: one consumer, one message at a time, and your throughput is one worker's throughput forever. The standard compromise is ordering within a partition key — all messages for user 42 go to one partition and are processed in order, while different users proceed in parallel. Nothing whatsoever orders user 42 against user 7, which is fine, because no invariant spanned them. The bill is that your consumer count can never usefully exceed your partition count, and a poorly distributed key recreates the single-consumer bottleneck with extra machinery on top.",
  ["agrees to global ordering without pricing it",
   "does not know parallelism is capped by partition count",
   "picks a low-cardinality partition key",
   "assumes the broker orders messages by default"],
  [("One message in a partition can never succeed. What happens?",
    "Without a delivery cap it retries forever, and because ordering is enforced it blocks everything behind it — one malformed message stops the entire pipeline. Cap the delivery count, then route it to a dead letter queue."),
   ("You have a DLQ. Are you done?",
    "Only if someone is alerted on it. A DLQ nobody monitors is a data-loss mechanism with extra steps, so the alert matters more than the queue does — and the threshold is anything above zero, because a message sitting there is work that silently did not happen.")],
  "06-messaging/queues/"),

Q("advanced",
  "When would you choose a log or stream over a queue?",
  "whether they know the confusion is expensive, and can name the single property that decides it",
  "When you might want the data twice. A queue deletes on consumption; a log retains, and each consumer holds an independent offset. That one difference decides replay: a bug found on Tuesday can be fixed by resetting the offset to Monday, whereas with a queue the messages are gone and the data is unrecoverable. It also changes how you scale — you add workers to a queue and partitions to a log. For anything resembling an event, the replay property alone usually justifies the log.",
  ["treats Kafka and SQS as interchangeable",
   "no mention of retention or of independent offsets",
   "says the log is simply faster",
   "cannot name a case where a plain queue is the better choice"],
  [("Your consumer group has 8 partitions and is lagging. Add workers?",
    "Not usefully beyond eight — consumer count cannot exceed partition count. You add partitions, and that is not free: it changes the key-to-partition mapping, which breaks per-key ordering across the boundary for keys that are in flight when you do it."),
   ("Your distributed trace stops at the queue boundary. Why does that matter more than it sounds?",
    "Because the queue is exactly where work goes missing. Propagate the correlation ID through message headers across asynchronous hops — a trace that ends at the broker is the most common gap in an otherwise good observability setup, and it is a gap sitting precisely over the component whose failures are least visible.")],
  "06-messaging/queues/"),
]


