API_DESIGN = [
Q("basic",
  "You are building a feed endpoint and a colleague writes LIMIT 20 OFFSET ?. What is wrong with it?",
  "whether they know offset pagination silently skips AND duplicates rows, and why no test they write will ever catch it",
  "OFFSET n means discard the first n rows of the result as it exists right now, and the result as it exists right now is not the result the caller was traversing. Insert a row above the window between page 1 and page 2 and the offset lands one short, so a row is delivered twice. Delete a row above the window and the offset lands one long, so a row is never delivered at all. Every individual response is correct for the state at the instant it was asked, which is why no participant is in a position to notice — and the bug is invisible in testing because test data does not move.",
  ["sees nothing wrong with it",
   "says it is fine because the tests pass",
   "cannot say which direction produces a skip and which a duplicate",
   "treats the skip and the duplicate as equally serious"],
  [("Which of the two is worse?",
    "The skip. A duplicate is at least visible to any client that de-duplicates by primary key; a skipped row is silent data loss with a clean success log. That is why a nightly warehouse sync losing rows looks one-directional — the duplicates were de-duped at load, so only the skips survive as visible symptoms."),
   ("A crawler walks to page 50,000. What does that cost you?",
    "OFFSET is O(offset), not O(limit): the engine cannot jump to row 1,000,000, it must produce and discard the preceding million every single time. LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000000 produces 1,000,020 rows to return 20, so the cost per page grows the deeper the caller goes. A crawler does not need to be malicious to take the database down; it merely needs to be thorough."),
   ("You switch to cursor pagination. What have you still not fixed?",
    "Sorting on a mutable key. Paginate by updated_at, a relevance score or a vote count and rows cross the boundary and are seen twice or never, exactly as with offset — the cursor is correct with respect to the ordering, and the ordering itself moved. You have also given up page numbers, because keyset traversal is inherently sequential; there is no jumping to page 7.")],
  "07-api-design/pagination/"),

Q("basic",
  "A client's request times out. Did the operation happen?",
  "whether they understand a timeout is an unknown outcome rather than a failure, and that the fix cannot live on the client",
  "Unknown, and that is the entire point. The caller's information after a timeout is identical whether the request never arrived or the reply never came back, so *retry only if it did not happen* is not implementable on the client at all — it is choosing between a possible duplicate and a possible loss, blind. That is what forces the fix onto the server as an idempotency key, stored with the result so a repeat replays the original response instead of re-executing. Exactly-once delivery does not exist; exactly-once *effect* does, and idempotency is how you get it.",
  ["says the client should check first, which is a race",
   "adds retries with no idempotency story",
   "stores the key but not the response body",
   "assumes a timeout or a 500 means it did not happen"],
  [("Walk me through the server side. Where does it usually go wrong?",
    "Insert the key with state in_progress and let the unique constraint arbitrate — not SELECT-then-INSERT, which is a read-modify-write race where two concurrent retries both find nothing and both execute. Then write the effect and the completed record with its response in one transaction. That single atomicity requirement is the mechanism; everything else is bookkeeping. A crash between the effect and the key write is precisely the bug it prevents."),
   ("How long do you keep the keys?",
    "Longer than the maximum age of anything that can retry into you: SDK backoff is seconds, mobile offline resume is hours, queue redelivery and a manual DLQ replay after an incident is days. Twenty-four hours is the common default and seven days is reasonable for financial endpoints — publish the number, because retention shorter than the retry horizon re-executes exactly the case you were protecting.")],
  "07-api-design/idempotency/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "You are adding a field to a response and the team proposes shipping it as /v2. Do you?",
  "whether they know additive changes are safe and that the hard part of versioning is deprecation, not versioning",
  "No. Adding a response field is not breaking, provided readers are tolerant — and if some caller's strict schema validator rejects unknown fields, the thing to fix is that validator, not to fork the whole API. Versioning does not remove old code, it accumulates it: teams who solved versioning by adding /v2 frequently still have v1 traffic five years later, and every live version is a permanent tax. Version the API rather than the endpoint, support N and N−1 and nothing older, and if your options table has no row for *do not version at all*, you have not finished thinking.",
  ["ships a new version for any change at all",
   "versions per endpoint, producing a matrix nobody can reason about",
   "announces a deprecation before instrumenting usage",
   "cannot name which changes are actually breaking"],
  [("Name three changes people believe are safe and are not.",
    "Adding an enum value breaks exhaustive matching in generated clients. Changing the default sort order or page size breaks anyone who relied on it. Changing an error code or error shape breaks error handling, which is client code too and is the least tested client code there is. Making a call ten times slower is breaking as well — client timeouts are part of your contract whether you agreed to them or not."),
   ("You are ready to remove v1 and three callers have never replied. What do you do?",
    "A brownout: return 410 Gone for five minutes at a fixed announced time, then for an hour a fortnight later. It converts silence into a support ticket from exactly the team you needed to talk to, and it is the only technique that finds the caller who never read anything. Before any of it, instrument — tag every request with caller identity, version and endpoint, because announcing a deprecation you cannot measure is how sunset dates become fictional.")],
  "07-api-design/versioning/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "A GraphQL query fetches 100 posts and each post's author. Your rate limiter records one request. What does the database see?",
  "whether they know N+1 here is a structural property of resolver-per-field execution rather than a bug somebody introduced",
  "101 queries — one for the posts and one per author. The cause is architectural rather than careless: a field resolver is invoked once per parent object and is handed only that parent, so it is structurally incapable of noticing that 99 near-identical queries are about to run beside it. DataLoader collapses them, but only within a single request, only if every resolver is written to use it, and never across concurrent requests. The N+1 is a permanent property of the execution model; the batching is a discipline. The signal that catches a new one is database queries per API request.",
  ["says DataLoader fixes it",
   "counts the client's single request as the load",
   "rate limits a GraphQL endpoint by request count",
   "has no metric relating database queries to API requests"],
  [("Your limiter allows 100 requests a minute and the caller stays under it. Why did the database fall over?",
    "Because request count is meaningless when request cost is unbounded. A query under 100 characters can be a combinatorial traversal of your entire social graph, and 100 of those a minute is not a modest load, it is an attack. A public GraphQL endpoint without query-cost limits is a denial-of-service endpoint you built and hosted for the attacker."),
   ("So what actually works?",
    "Persisted queries: clients register their operations ahead of time and the endpoint refuses anything not on the allow-list. Depth limits and complexity scoring help, but that is the one that works. Notice what you have then got back — a finite set of known operations you can cost, cache and rate limit, which is what REST gave you for free. Locking GraphQL down enough to operate it safely removes most of the reason you adopted it.")],
  "07-api-design/rest-grpc-graphql/"),

Q("advanced",
  "You moved internal traffic to gRPC. One of six pods sits at 90% CPU while the others idle at 15%, and the load balancer reports a perfectly even split. Explain.",
  "whether they know the layer you balance at must match the layer your protocol multiplexes at",
  "HTTP/2 multiplexes every call over one long-lived TCP connection, and a connection-level balancer makes exactly one routing decision, at connect time. So the split is genuinely even in connections and wildly uneven in requests — the dashboard is telling the truth about the wrong unit. This is structural rather than a misconfiguration. The fixes, in increasing order of correctness: set max_connection_age so connections churn, put an L7 proxy in that balances per request, or do client-side load balancing with service discovery.",
  ["blames the pod, the autoscaler or a noisy neighbour",
   "adds more pods, which does not change the mapping",
   "reads the even connection split as evidence the balancer is fine",
   "no mention of HTTP/2 multiplexing"],
  [("Same architecture, and a downstream call hangs. What is gRPC's default timeout?",
    "There is none. A gRPC call with no deadline waits indefinitely, so a hung callee holds the caller's thread forever. Set a deadline on every call and propagate the remaining budget downstream — slow is worse than down, and this particular default makes slow unbounded."),
   ("The team justified gRPC on payload size. Is that its strongest argument?",
    "No, it is the weakest of its four. Protobuf is several times smaller than equivalent JSON and then gzip closes most of that gap on the wire. The durable wins are the mandatory schema, generated clients that fail at compile time rather than in production, native bidirectional streaming, and cheap parsing at high call rates.")],
  "07-api-design/rest-grpc-graphql/"),
]


