SECURITY = [
Q("basic",
  "What is the difference between authentication and authorisation, and which of the two causes more breaches?",
  "whether they can name the specific failure the conflation produces, rather than reciting the dictionary distinction",
  "Authentication asks who is this; authorisation asks what may they do. Authorisation causes far more breaches, and specifically object-level authorisation. The signature failure: a team builds excellent authentication — hashed passwords, MFA, short-lived tokens — bolts it on as middleware, and ships an API where any logged-in user reads any other user's invoice by changing a number in the URL. Every control worked, nobody was impersonated, and the data left anyway. That is OWASP API1, Broken Object Level Authorization, and it is first on that list because it is first in reality.",
  ["gives the dictionary definitions and stops there",
   "answers authentication, because passwords feel like the risky part",
   "uses the word auth for both in the same breath",
   "cannot say where in the request the object check must happen"],
  [("Where in the request must that check live, and why can a gateway not do it?",
    "At the point where the object is loaded. A gateway can answer *may a logged-in user call this endpoint*, because that is a property of the route. It cannot answer *does invoice 8842 belong to user 7*, because nothing outside the data layer knows which object the request will touch — and its presence makes teams believe it did."),
   ("How do you make forgetting that check impossible rather than merely unlikely?",
    "Design so that absence of the check fails closed. Scope the query so the query *is* the authorisation — current_user.orders.find(id) rather than Order.find(id) — or use a repository layer that requires a subject argument, or row-level security in the database. Relying on every developer remembering an if statement on every endpoint forever is not a control; it is a hope with a code review attached."),
   ("One token produces 4,000 authorisation failures in an hour across thousands of distinct object ids. Is that an incident?",
    "Yes, even though every control held. That is enumeration in progress, and the 403 rate per principal is the most useful security signal an API has — almost nobody alerts on it. Suspend the credential, capture the full request log before retention expires, and check which object ids returned 200 in the same window: that is your exposure list.")],
  "12-security/api-security/"),

Q("basic",
  "Why can you not revoke a JWT?",
  "whether the revocation window is understood as the thing they bought, and whether they will describe their own system honestly",
  "Because verification is a local signature check with no call back to the issuer — which is the entire speed advantage, and precisely why the gate cannot be told the holder was ejected. A user disabled at 10:00 holding a 60-minute token issued at 09:55 keeps access until 10:55, and nothing in any log looks wrong: the cause is a missing arrow rather than a broken one. Every strategy that revokes quickly is server state, which is what JWTs were adopted to avoid. The best value by a distance is a per-user token version — one integer per user — which revokes all of that user's tokens at once.",
  ["calls the system stateless while maintaining a denylist",
   "proposes introspection on every request without noticing they have rebuilt sessions",
   "has no stated revocation window anywhere",
   "cannot name an option between short expiry and a full session store"],
  [("Product wants suspension effective within one second, and the architecture document says stateless. Reconcile those.",
    "You cannot have both, so state the number instead of the adjective. A per-user token version in a replicated cache gives near-immediate revocation bounded by cache propagation — measure it and publish it, and if it is 200 ms you meet the requirement. Every JWT is stale on arrival; the only question is by how much, and a stated bound beats a claimed zero."),
   ("Which validation step do people skip, and what does skipping it cost?",
    "aud and iss. Skipping them turns a perfectly valid token minted for somewhere else into an authorisation bypass — a staging token working against production is the friendly version of that bug. Also pin the algorithm server-side and never let the token choose, or alg:none and HS256/RS256 confusion both work. In real code review the mistake that outnumbers all of them is calling decode instead of verify; grep for it.")],
  "12-security/jwt/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "Your login endpoint uses bcrypt at cost 12. Someone starts sending 5,000 password attempts a second. What happens?",
  "whether they see the login endpoint as a CPU-bound service, and whether they know the two tempting fixes are both wrong",
  "You go down without a single password being guessed. Cost 12 is roughly 250 ms of CPU per verification, so a 40-core fleet does about 160 verifications a second — at 5,000 attempts a second the queue is unbounded, CPU pins, and already-authenticated users start timing out. The asymmetry is about 100,000 to 1 in the attacker's favour: a few hundred bytes buys 250 ms of your CPU, and they do not need to guess correctly even once. Your login endpoint is a CPU-bound service wearing a web endpoint's clothes, and nobody capacity-plans it that way. Rate limit before the key derivation function, not after, and isolate the login path so it cannot starve everything else.",
  ["lowers the cost factor under incident pressure",
   "autoscales, which scales into the attacker's bill",
   "rate limits after the password hash rather than before",
   "treats login as an ordinary I/O-bound endpoint"],
  [("Why is lowering the cost factor the wrong reflex?",
    "Because it permanently weakens every stored hash to solve a temporary traffic problem, and it never gets raised again. Decide the floor in advance and write it down. Tune the cost to a time budget rather than a constant — roughly 100-250 ms per verification on production hardware, re-measured yearly — and monitor KDF verification latency, because if it suddenly halves, somebody lowered it to make a graph look better."),
   ("You rate limit per account. What does that miss?",
    "Password spraying: one common password against many accounts is invisible to a per-account limit. Per-IP misses it too, because a botnet rotates addresses freely and IPv6 hands a single host a whole /64, while a corporate NAT presents thousands of real users as one address. Neither dimension catches spraying — for that you need the global failure ratio.")],
  "12-security/authentication/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "A partner integration is failing with a CORS error. The quickest fix reflects the Origin header back with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials set to true. Do you ship it?",
  "whether they know CORS is a browser rule rather than an access control, and can recognise the single most dangerous configuration in the area",
  "No. That combination lets any website in the world make authenticated requests as your logged-in users and read the responses. It is strictly worse than the CSRF it resembles, because CSRF can normally only write blind and this hands over the response body as well. It is almost always introduced by someone trying to make a CORS error go away quickly. The fix is an explicit allowlist compared exactly — the literal string https://app.partner.com, not a prefix match and not a regex with an unescaped dot.",
  ["ships it because it unblocks the partner today",
   "believes CORS protects the API",
   "reads a CORS error as proof the request was blocked",
   "matches Origin with a prefix check or a regex"],
  [("The partner says they got a CORS error so nothing happened. Are they right?",
    "No. CORS is evaluated after your code has run — even in the browser lane the write already happened, and CORS withheld the response rather than the effect. And curl, Postman, a mobile app and any server-side script ignore it entirely: it is a rule a browser applies to its own scripts, and it grants nothing on your server."),
   ("The same integration leaks an API key into a public repository. It is reverted and force-pushed within ten minutes. Is the incident over?",
    "No. A force-push rewrites the branch pointer; it does not reliably destroy the object. The blob remains in every clone, the reflog, forks, CI caches and the code review tool's stored diff. Revocation is what ends the incident and deleting the line is cosmetic — and the real finding is how long rotation takes, because rotation speed matters far more than secret complexity.")],
  "12-security/api-security/"),

Q("advanced",
  "Your login-with-a-provider flow takes the OAuth access token, calls /userinfo, and signs the user in. What is wrong?",
  "whether they know OAuth 2.0 is delegated authorisation rather than login, and can walk the token substitution attack end to end",
  "You have built a token substitution vulnerability. An attacker registers an ordinary-looking application with the same provider, gets a victim to sign in to it, and now holds a valid access token for the victim's account. They replay it at your login endpoint; you call /userinfo, are truthfully told the token belongs to the victim, and sign the attacker in as them. Nothing was forged — the token was valid, it was simply never meant for you, and an access token carries nothing that says who it was minted for. The fix is the OIDC ID token, whose aud claim names the client. Valid and intended-for-me are different questions, and only the second is a security check.",
  ["cannot distinguish OAuth 2.0 from OIDC",
   "treats a /userinfo response as authentication",
   "has the client parsing the access token",
   "says scopes are the permission model"],
  [("Your team says PKCE is unnecessary because you are a confidential client with a secret. Respond.",
    "The client secret protects the token request; PKCE protects the code, and the code travels through the browser where the secret gives it no protection at all. The code leaks through Referer headers, browser history, proxy logs and any open redirect on your own domain. The secret is one static value shared across the whole fleet, quite possibly in a container image and three CI systems, whereas PKCE adds a per-request secret. OAuth 2.1 requires it for every client, because conditional security requirements are the ones that get dropped."),
   ("You need immediate revocation at 40,000 rps, and introspection per request would put the authorisation server on your read path. What do you do?",
    "Shorten access tokens to about five minutes and make the refresh path the enforcement point, plus a per-user token version held in a local cache. Then be honest in writing about the residual window — a stated five-second window everyone understands beats a claimed zero that is actually fifteen minutes. And note the failure mode you just bought: short tokens plus a dead authorisation server logs out the world in fifteen minutes.")],
  "12-security/oauth/"),

Q("advanced",
  "A PDF report endpoint burns 4 seconds of CPU, is uncached, and is available to any authenticated user. You have 200 cores and a DDoS scrubbing provider. Are you protected?",
  "whether they can compute cost asymmetry and see that essentially none of the perimeter helps against a logic attack",
  "No, and almost nothing you have bought helps. 200 cores divided by 4 seconds is 50 reports a second at absolute saturation, so a sustained 25 rps consumes half the fleet and 50 rps takes the service down. One authenticated user on a home connection produces 50 rps of small POSTs without effort — well under a megabit of upload. Scrubbing sees nothing because the traffic is tiny, the edge cache cannot help because the responses are per-user, and a WAF sees well-formed authenticated requests. The number that matters is not requests per second but cost asymmetry: what one request costs the attacker versus what it costs you.",
  ["points at the scrubbing provider",
   "expects the WAF to catch it",
   "proposes autoscaling, which is a payment plan rather than a defence",
   "reasons in requests per second instead of CPU-seconds per request"],
  [("Your rate limiter allows 100 requests a minute. Does that fix it?",
    "No. A flat limit allows 100 reports a minute, which is still fatal. The limit has to be cost-weighted, so that one report consumes the same quota as several hundred ordinary requests. The real fix is in the application: accept the request, enqueue it, return a job id, and cap per-user concurrency at one."),
   ("Suppose instead it is a genuine 400 Gbps volumetric flood. Why is your firewall irrelevant?",
    "Because the firewall sits behind the link that is already full. You cannot rate-limit a packet you have already paid to receive — if your uplink is 10 Gbps, 97% of legitimate packets never arrived either, and what your software decides afterwards changes nothing. That layer has to be handled upstream by anycast and scrubbing, because attack capacity is a shared-cost good and buying your own would cost more than the company.")],
  "12-security/ddos/"),
]


