OBSERVABILITY = [
Q("basic",
  "To chase one customer complaint, an engineer adds user_id as a label on the HTTP request metric. The deploy goes out at 14:00. Predict 14:30.",
  "whether they know metric labels multiply, and that the backend dies during the very incident that prompted the label",
  "The metrics backend runs out of memory and you go blind, plausibly just before the next real incident. Labels multiply into time series: method, status and endpoint might be 4 x 6 x 50 = 1,200 series, and a million user ids makes that 1.2 billion. The unbounded label did not add a million, it multiplied the 1,200 that were fine. Metric labels must be bounded; per-request identity belongs in logs or traces, which are built for unbounded cardinality and pay for it with sampling and retention limits.",
  ["sees no problem because it is only one more label",
   "plans to remove it later",
   "cannot say why logs or traces are the right home",
   "does not realise series count is a product"],
  [("A colleague adds the same label to a metric that has no other dimensions. Same severity?",
    "No, and that is the subtle part: series count is a product, so the damage a label does depends on every other label already present. A million series is survivable; 1.2 billion is not — and nothing at the call site distinguishes the two. It is also why removing *other* labels is a genuine mitigation."),
   ("Which pillar answers how often, and which answers why this one request?",
    "Metrics detect, traces localise, logs explain. Metrics cannot tell you which request or why; logs cannot tell you how often at any sane cost; traces cannot tell you anything at all about unsampled requests. Each pillar's blind spot is the next one's strength, which is why none of them substitutes for another.")],
  "11-observability/"),

Q("basic",
  "Your CPU alert fires at 80% every night during the batch job. What is wrong with the alert?",
  "whether they alert on symptoms or causes, and whether they understand what a noisy pager actually costs",
  "It is a cause alert rather than a symptom alert. CPU above 80% may or may not matter — a healthy batch job can legitimately pin CPU for an hour — whereas p99 latency above one second is a symptom, meaning users are affected. Cause alerts are how you train people to ignore the pager, and a team that has learned its pager is usually noise will miss the one that is not. Every page must be actionable: if the runbook says *check whether it recovers*, it should have been a ticket.",
  ["raises the threshold to 90% and calls it fixed",
   "adds a time-of-day exclusion instead of changing the signal",
   "cannot propose a symptom-based alternative",
   "has no sense of what the page is costing the rotation"],
  [("What should page, then?",
    "User impact, and error budget burning fast. Three gates: are users affected now or certainly within the hour; is there something a human can actually do right now; and does waiting until business hours cost anything. Only the third one pages — read it as a filter rather than a router, where dashboard is the default destination and a page is the rare thing you argue your way to."),
   ("Give me one rule that catches both a fast catastrophe and a slow leak.",
    "Burn rate. A single failed request should never page; consuming 5% of the monthly error budget in an hour should. One rule covers both shapes, and it is the single biggest improvement available to most alerting setups.")],
  "11-observability/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "Define SLI, SLO and error budget — and tell me what you do when the budget is unspent.",
  "whether the error budget is a two-directional mechanism to them, or merely a stick",
  "The SLI is the measured indicator, for example the fraction of requests served under 300 ms. The SLO is the target for it: 99.9% over 30 days. The error budget is 1 minus the SLO, which at 99.9% over 30 days is about 43 minutes of failure you are permitted. If the budget is exhausted you stop shipping features and fix reliability. If it is unspent you are being too cautious and should ship faster — both directions matter, and teams usually implement only the second.",
  ["treats the error budget purely as a brake",
   "proposes raising the SLO when the budget goes unspent",
   "sets an SLO of 100%",
   "cannot convert an SLO into minutes"],
  [("Why not simply raise the SLO instead of shipping faster?",
    "Because it removes the slack without any user noticing an improvement, and makes the mechanism harsher for no return. Set the SLO below what users currently experience but above what they would complain about. An SLO of 100% is not a target, it is a refusal to make a decision, and it guarantees the budget is always exhausted so the mechanism does nothing at all."),
   ("Of the four golden signals, which one do people omit?",
    "Saturation, and it is the one that predicts. Latency and errors tell you it has already gone wrong; a connection pool at 90% or a queue depth climbing tells you it is about to. That is the difference between an alert you can act on and a post-mortem.")],
  "11-observability/"),

Q("intermediate",
  "The team wants to trace 100% of requests so that nothing is ever missed. Do you approve it?",
  "whether they can decline more observability, and whether they know tail-based sampling exists",
  "No. Tracing every request at scale costs more than the system it observes, and the marginal benefit is close to zero — you keep millions of identical healthy traces in order to catch the few that are not. Sample traces and keep all errors. Head-based sampling decides at the start, typically at 1%: cheap, and it misses rare failures. Tail-based sampling decides after seeing the whole trace and keeps the slow and failed ones — it costs more and keeps what you actually need.",
  ["approves because more data is always better",
   "does not distinguish head-based from tail-based sampling",
   "no mention of cost at all",
   "would add dashboards before adding the first alert"],
  [("Where would you spend that budget instead?",
    "On the first alert, and on structured logging. The first alert is worth more than the third dashboard, and a dashboard with no owner and no question is decoration — nobody is watching a dashboard at 3am. Structured logs cost the same as unstructured ones and are the difference between usable and unusable during an incident."),
   ("Your logging pipeline runs through the service that is down. What have you built?",
    "A system that goes blind exactly when it is needed. The observability path must not share fate with the system it observes — the same reason a cardinality explosion is so damaging is that it kills the metrics backend during the incident that prompted the label. Also worth pricing honestly: a self-hosted stack means you now operate a distributed system in order to watch your distributed system.")],
  "11-observability/"),

Q("advanced",
  "You compute p99 latency per host and average those across the fleet for the dashboard. What is wrong?",
  "a small, precise error that separates people who have built dashboards from people who have read about them — percentiles do not average",
  "Percentiles do not average. The mean of per-host p99s is not the fleet's p99 and has no statistical interpretation at all: a host serving ten requests contributes as much as one serving ten million, and the fleet's true p99 can sit outside the range of the per-host values entirely. Aggregate the underlying distribution instead — sum histogram buckets across hosts, then compute the quantile once. And track p50, p99 and p99.9 separately, per endpoint, splitting successful requests from failed ones, because fast errors otherwise flatter the number.",
  ["sees nothing wrong with it",
   "offers the max of the per-host p99s without noting that is a different statistic",
   "no mention of histograms or of aggregating the distribution",
   "does not separate error latency from success latency"],
  [("Your traces show a 900 ms gap with no span in it. Where do you look?",
    "At an asynchronous boundary first, and at the clock second. Trace context dropped at a queue hop is the most common gap of all, and clock skew across hosts makes cross-host timestamps unreliable — rely on span durations rather than wall-clock differences, and make sure NTP is actually running."),
   ("You have excellent dashboards and a 40-pages-a-week rotation. What is the real problem?",
    "Alert fatigue, and it is a correctness problem rather than a comfort one — the responder has learned that the pager is noise and will miss the one that is not. Move cause alerts to tickets, page only on burn rate and user impact, put a runbook link on every page, and delete the dashboards nobody owns.")],
  "11-observability/"),
]


